WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.260
From breathtaking action sequences&nbsp;
that are arguably better than those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:07.980
in the first film to the continued&nbsp;
use of tired and harmful tropes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:13.140
here are the 6 best and 6 worst things&nbsp;
about "Avatar: The Way of Water" ranked.

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:17.880
James Cameron is well-known for being at the&nbsp;
forefront of advancing the tech behind visual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:23.520
effects in film, especially with regards to using&nbsp;
computer-generated images — or CGI. While there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:28.800
have been computer-generated visual effects in&nbsp;
films going as far back as 1973 with the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:34.320
"Westworld", Cameron is famous for continuing to&nbsp;
push the technology further with each successive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:39.240
project. For instance, in 1989's "The Abyss,"&nbsp;
Cameron used — at the time — state-of-the-art&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:44.940
CGI to create the now-famous "water tentacle"&nbsp;
with digitally-mapped 3D faces of the actors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:50.100
He then created the first partially-CG main&nbsp;
character in Robert Patrick's liquid metal T-1000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:55.020
form in "Terminator 2: Judgment Day," and later&nbsp;
pushed the tech once more in 2009's "Avatar."

00:00:55.020 --> 00:01:00.480
It must be said that the CGI in "Avatar: The Way&nbsp;
of Water" is indeed pretty impressive. While the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:04.560
visuals in the original "Avatar" were cutting&nbsp;
edge at the time, they — like a lot of CGI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:09.420
effects in films before it — have shown their age&nbsp;
a bit. However, the advancement in visual effects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:14.340
technology between the first "Avatar" and "The Way&nbsp;
of Water" is palpable. It's especially noticeable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:18.480
when the film focuses on the Na'vi's facial&nbsp;
features, or anything involving the physics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:23.100
of water. Not only that, but there are many&nbsp;
effects-heavy scenes in the film, which — coupled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:27.112
with filming in a higher frame rate — makes&nbsp;
it feel like you're literally on Pandora.

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:32.280
Despite the previously mentioned moments of&nbsp;
special effects greatness, there are also some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:37.200
VFX shots that were obviously given more care&nbsp;
than others. Despite a long-gestating 13-year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:41.040
gap between "Avatar: The Way of Water" and&nbsp;
the original "Avatar," there's still not an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:45.660
infinite amount of time to design, plan, and&nbsp;
render each shot — especially for a film that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:51.060
is mostly CG-animated. This is all exacerbated&nbsp;
by the fact that director James Cameron insisted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:54.900
on creating new tech specifically for&nbsp;
the film, but also meant that research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:59.100
and development for the new tech inevitably cut&nbsp;
into rendering times for the individual shots.

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:04.080
This is not to mention the lack of unionization&nbsp;
in the VFX industry that leads to artist burn-out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:08.280
fast turnarounds, and outsourcing some&nbsp;
shots to VFX houses offering the lowest bid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:11.880
rather than the best quality. This&nbsp;
is admittedly an industry-wide issue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:16.080
and not just relegated to "Avatar: The Way of&nbsp;
Water" — but still. It needs to be mentioned.

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:21.600
James Cameron has had a prolific career as an&nbsp;
A-list director, and one of the main things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:26.520
he's known for is his amazing action scenes. Even&nbsp;
his epic period drama "Titanic" — which focused&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:31.320
most of its three-hour-plus runtime on a budding&nbsp;
romance — still had an intense, pulse-pounding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:36.000
40-minute climax, featuring some of the best&nbsp;
action sequences in cinema history to this day.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:38.173
"Ahhhhhhh!"

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:46.020
"Avatar: The Way of Water" is no exception.&nbsp;
There are so many great action set pieces in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:49.980
the film that rival, and arguably&nbsp;
surpass, the 2009 original. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:53.340
includes an exciting futuristic train&nbsp;
heist near the beginning of the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:58.560
a scene where the evil RDA corporation chases&nbsp;
down a herd of the sentient whale-like tulkun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:03.660
and the climax on the ocean where Jake Sully&nbsp;
leads his family and the marine-based Metkayina&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:06.960
tribe against a newly-resurrected&nbsp;
Colonel Quaritch and his gang.

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:09.900
The finale in particular is a&nbsp;
really breathtaking sequence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:14.340
and showcases why Cameron is the top of the&nbsp;
game in terms of action genre cinema. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:18.480
blocking is clear and precise, the spatial&nbsp;
orientation is always legible, and there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:23.400
no overuse of either shaky-cam or the overdone&nbsp;
Zack Snyder-esque slow-down/ramp-up technique.

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:28.020
James Cameron is no stranger to&nbsp;
having long runtimes for his films.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:31.980
1997's "Titanic," for instance, was&nbsp;
three hours and 14 minutes. Even the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:35.880
original "Avatar" wasn't anemic either,&nbsp;
with a two-hour and 41-minute runtime.

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:40.380
While "Avatar: The Way of Water" may not be the&nbsp;
longest film in Cameron's career — clocking in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:44.460
at three hours and 12 minutes — it's still one&nbsp;
of the longest mainstream Hollywood films in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:48.960
recent memory, with only "Avengers: Endgame"&nbsp;
coming close at a minute over three hours.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:53.700
This is not to say that long runtimes are&nbsp;
inherently bad — far from it. This year's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:58.380
blockbuster Tollywood period action-adventure&nbsp;
film "RRR" ran for three hours and seven minutes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:02.640
and is one of 2022's most financially and&nbsp;
critically successful films worldwide.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:07.140
However, "RRR," along with most of the&nbsp;
three-hour-plus epics from decades past,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:11.760
at least had intermissions, which is not the&nbsp;
case for modern theatrical films. To be fair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:15.420
James Cameron has suggested seeing the movie&nbsp;
multiple times if going to the bathroom is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:19.680
a problem ... though that seems to be a&nbsp;
solution that's more than a bit self-serving.

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:24.240
Whatever critics might say about the&nbsp;
quality — or lack thereof — concerning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:27.540
the visual effects in "Avatar: The Way of&nbsp;
Water," the art direction on the film is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:32.220
at least consistently phenomenal regardless.&nbsp;
Honestly, the style of the film truly evokes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:36.060
the aesthetic of the old Lisa Frank Trapper&nbsp;
Keepers from the '80s and '90s — and that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:40.620
a good thing. The film is just so full of&nbsp;
vibrant, saturated, and bright colors that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:44.280
nothing else that has come out in cinemas&nbsp;
for the last few years looks quite like it.

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:48.780
The production design is frankly next level,&nbsp;
and stands head-and-shoulders above a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:53.160
of other big-budget sci-fi films of the same&nbsp;
caliber. There's almost no comparison to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:56.880
amount of detail that James Cameron has put&nbsp;
into building every single nook and cranny&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:02.280
of Pandora. This includes the idyllic coastal&nbsp;
Metkayinan villages and the militaristic RDA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:06.720
corporate facility that contrasts greatly with the&nbsp;
naturalistic Pandoran landscape surrounding it.

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:11.520
The various cool and exotic alien creatures in&nbsp;
the sequel are often a sight to behold as well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:15.540
in particular the sentient whale-like&nbsp;
tulkuns, or the "skimwing," creatures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:20.768
that the Metkayinan Na'vi tribe ride which are&nbsp;
similar to the flying ikran from the first film.

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:24.300
One of the biggest issues with&nbsp;
the first "Avatar" was the story's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:28.560
inherent "white savior" issue. While pretty&nbsp;
self-explanatory, the term "white savior"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:33.300
refers to an extremely outdated — and arguably&nbsp;
white supremacist — narrative trope in fiction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:37.500
where a white person joins a group of marginalized&nbsp;
people, and instantly becomes the best of them.

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:40.620
This fits the story of the&nbsp;
original "Avatar" to a tee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:45.360
as a white man named Jake Sully becomes part of&nbsp;
the Indigenous-coded alien Na'vi tribe on Pandora,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:49.680
and ends up not only being the best of them,&nbsp;
but also literally becoming their leader.

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:54.600
To be clear, this is not to say that "Avatar" is&nbsp;
intentionally hateful or anything like that. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:58.380
it's not great when the film nonetheless&nbsp;
reinforces harmful narrative tropes like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.380 --> 00:06:02.460
"the white savior" and "the noble savage,"&nbsp;
which fit the depiction of the Na'vi as well.

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:06.420
Unfortunately, "Avatar: The Way of Water"&nbsp;
does little to alleviate these problems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:10.620
and in fact compounds them. Jake ends up&nbsp;
leading an attack on the humans with not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.620 --> 00:06:15.180
just his own family, but also commanding the&nbsp;
literal king of the Metkayina Na'vi tribe as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:19.980
well. His negligent actions also lead directly&nbsp;
to the devastation of many Metkayina villages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:23.040
which never amounts to much in&nbsp;
the way of consequences for him.

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:27.900
James Cameron is known for making some of&nbsp;
the best sequels of all time, whether it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:32.460
following up Ridley Scott's classic sci-fi/horror&nbsp;
film "Alien" with one of the best sci-fi/action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:37.260
flicks ever in "Aliens," or continuing his own&nbsp;
"Terminator" tale with "Terminator 2: Judgment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:41.940
Day," that somehow made Arnold Schwarzenegger's&nbsp;
deadly Terminator loveable enough to cry over.

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:44.700
"Why the hell'd you do that?"

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:45.780
"Because you told me to."

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:50.580
In both cases, Cameron was able to find a way&nbsp;
to turn those original stories on their heads,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:54.300
but also made sure that what made those&nbsp;
films work in the first place remained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:59.340
intact — such as keeping the xenomorphs scary,&nbsp;
or letting both "Terminator" films be explosive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:02.340
bloody, and thrilling. The same&nbsp;
goes for "Avatar: The Way of Water."

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:06.240
Cameron keeps what worked in the first&nbsp;
film — the amazingly-rendered environments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:10.260
the anti-corporate message, and the deep&nbsp;
spiritual mythology — and adds some new,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:14.340
much-appreciated wrinkles. One of the most&nbsp;
narratively satisfying additions to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:19.140
sequel is the inclusion of the Metkayina clan, an&nbsp;
aquatic-based Na'vi tribe living on the beaches&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.140 --> 00:07:23.640
near Pandora's oceans. The initial tension&nbsp;
between Jake and the Metakayina, as well as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:27.780
a sense that the Na'vi tribes have long-standing&nbsp;
prejudices against each other, makes the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:32.820
feel much more real and nuanced. It also raises&nbsp;
the stakes by making fighting off the humans that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.820 --> 00:07:37.082
much harder, which also reflects Indigenous&nbsp;
struggles against colonization in our world.

00:07:38.100 --> 00:07:43.080
Stephen Lang is a great actor. In many of his&nbsp;
roles, he's able to effortlessly exude extreme&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:47.940
intensity and menace, including his performance as&nbsp;
"Avatar" main antagonist Colonel Miles Quaritch.

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:49.670
"You crossed the line."

00:07:52.159 --> 00:07:55.860
In that first film, Quaritch thematically&nbsp;
represents the military arm of capitalistic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.860 --> 00:08:00.840
imperialism, as the massive corporation&nbsp;
RDA contracts the Earth military to aid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.840 --> 00:08:04.560
in Pandora's colonization — which is&nbsp;
based around wiping out or enslaving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:09.000
the native Na'vi population to ease efforts&nbsp;
in exploiting the planet's special resource,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:13.200
unobtainium. Lang does a great job in the&nbsp;
role: he's just the right mix of arrogant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:16.620
strong-willed, and scary to be a&nbsp;
worthy adversary for the heroes.

00:08:16.620 --> 00:08:21.180
However, he's also a bit one-dimensional.&nbsp;
It's fine for the story that "Avatar" tells,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.180 --> 00:08:25.140
which is more about Jake Sully discovering&nbsp;
and learning to love Pandora and the Na'vi;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.140 --> 00:08:29.400
it makes sense that more screen time and&nbsp;
narrative real estate is given to that plotline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:33.900
rather than developing Quaritch as a character.&nbsp;
But unlike fun or interesting villains like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.900 --> 00:08:38.520
Loki or Hannibal Lecter, Quaritch is just not&nbsp;
compelling enough to bring back. As written,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.520 --> 00:08:41.220
he was always just a stereotypical military guy.

00:08:41.220 --> 00:08:45.600
To be fair, Cameron does attempt to add layers&nbsp;
to Quaritch in "Avatar: The Way of Water" —&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:50.340
particularly his relationship to his left-behind&nbsp;
son, Spider. Still though, even that's hacky and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.340 --> 00:08:55.158
cliché in its own way, hearkening back to the fact&nbsp;
that so many villains have daddy issues already.

00:08:56.220 --> 00:09:00.900
Like any good spectacle-filled action epic, there&nbsp;
are a lot of applause-worthy moments sprinkled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.900 --> 00:09:05.340
throughout "Avatar: The Way of Water." However,&nbsp;
none are as crowd-pleasing as the scene where a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.340 --> 00:09:09.900
banished tulkun — a massive, sentient whale-like&nbsp;
creature that is a vital part of Metkayina&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.900 --> 00:09:15.120
culture — jumps out of the ocean to completely&nbsp;
decimate an RDA-funded tulkun-hunting vessel.

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:19.620
Honestly, the banished tulkun character – who&nbsp;
is befriended by Jake Sully's youngest son,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.620 --> 00:09:24.540
Lo'ak — is a highlight of the film in general.&nbsp;
The bond that forms between Lo'ak and the banished&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.540 --> 00:09:29.160
tulkun has all the hallmarks of a classic "A&nbsp;
Boy and His X" story, but done extremely well.

00:09:29.160 --> 00:09:33.600
The subplot takes its time to tease out&nbsp;
the banished tulkun's tragic backstory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:36.960
as Lo'ak initially discovers the&nbsp;
tulkun covered in injuries with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:40.740
remnants of Earth tech embedded in its&nbsp;
hide. The film's extended runtime also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.740 --> 00:09:43.800
allows for the tulkun and Lo'ak's&nbsp;
deep friendship to grow naturally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.800 --> 00:09:48.720
and makes the tulkun's redemption in helping the&nbsp;
Na'vi fight the humans that much more satisfying.

00:09:49.860 --> 00:09:53.940
As an actress, Sigourney Weaver is a&nbsp;
nerdy fan-favorite, starring in many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.940 --> 00:09:57.600
classic sci-fi genre staples over the&nbsp;
years such as "Galaxy Quest," all but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.600 --> 00:10:01.500
one "Ghostbusters" film, and — of course —&nbsp;
as Ellen Ripley in the "Alien" franchise.

00:10:01.500 --> 00:10:06.420
Weaver returned to star in Cameron's "Avatar"&nbsp;
in 2009, where she plays Dr. Grace Augustine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.420 --> 00:10:10.380
a highly-regarded xenobotanist on&nbsp;
Pandora who is also the head of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.380 --> 00:10:14.820
"Avatar Program" that puts human minds&nbsp;
into Na'vi bodies. Dr. Augustine is also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.820 --> 00:10:18.360
sympathetic to Pandora and the Na'vi,&nbsp;
unlike a lot of her human colleagues.

00:10:18.360 --> 00:10:24.840
"You need to listen to what she says. You&nbsp;
need to see the forest through her eyes."

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:29.220
Unfortunately, she dies before the end of the&nbsp;
first film, after an attempt to resurrect her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.220 --> 00:10:33.180
at the Tree of Souls fails. Surprisingly,&nbsp;
like Lang, Weaver returns in "Avatar:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.180 --> 00:10:38.460
The Way of Water." However, unlike Lang, Weaver&nbsp;
returns as a new Na'vi character named Kiri,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.460 --> 00:10:43.500
the adopted teen daughter of Jake Sully and&nbsp;
Neytiri. Unfortunately, despite her best efforts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.500 --> 00:10:47.460
Weaver playing a teenager is simply too&nbsp;
distracting to work. Weaver, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.460 --> 00:10:51.180
is an amazing actress, but it's still not&nbsp;
enough to hide the fact that she is starring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.180 --> 00:10:55.560
alongside actual young adults as her siblings.&nbsp;
Though her Na'vi form looks like a young,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.560 --> 00:11:00.660
blue Sigourney Weaver, the voice doesn't, and it&nbsp;
comes off as unsettling more than anything else.

00:11:01.860 --> 00:11:06.540
Whatever James Cameron's faults are as a&nbsp;
storyteller, his lack of subtlety is not one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.540 --> 00:11:10.560
of them. Say what you will about "Avatar,"&nbsp;
but the themes of anti-capitalism are very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:14.880
clear and well-defined in that film. The&nbsp;
audience unambiguously witnesses the violent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.880 --> 00:11:18.660
devastating effects that careless,&nbsp;
untethered capitalistic greed has on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.660 --> 00:11:22.800
the beauty and balance of nature, and how&nbsp;
its lust for infinite growth also leads to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:26.880
the genocide of entire cultures, like the&nbsp;
Na'vi — or Indigenous tribes in real life.

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:31.560
This anti-capitalist messaging is thankfully&nbsp;
maintained in "Avatar: The Way of Water." In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.560 --> 00:11:36.000
that message is expanded upon in the new film.&nbsp;
We find out that there's a whole operation run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:41.040
by the RDA, where they round up and slaughter&nbsp;
entire families of tulkun simply to extract a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:46.260
single vial of brain fluid. Meanwhile, the corpse&nbsp;
of the whale-sized creature is left to rot in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.260 --> 00:11:51.360
the ocean. This is reminiscent of the wasteful&nbsp;
and inhumane whaling practices on Earth today.

00:11:52.440 --> 00:11:57.060
One of the best characters from the original&nbsp;
"Avatar" film was the proud and strong Na'vi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.060 --> 00:12:01.740
warrior Neytiri, with Zoe Saldaña of "Guardians&nbsp;
of the Galaxy" fame doing the vocal and motion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.740 --> 00:12:06.840
capture performance for her. In fact, Neytiri's&nbsp;
beauty, strength, and bravery are the main reasons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.840 --> 00:12:11.100
that Sam Worthington's Jake Sully switches&nbsp;
sides, deciding to protect the Na'vi from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.100 --> 00:12:15.720
the invading human army. She is Jake's — and&nbsp;
the audience's — guide to the wondrous world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.720 --> 00:12:19.920
of Pandora, and her and Jake's love story is&nbsp;
the emotional crux of the entire first film.

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:22.540
"But this woman must also choose me."

00:12:26.935 --> 00:12:28.640
"She already has."

00:12:29.100 --> 00:12:34.320
Unfortunately, in "Avatar: The Way of Water,"&nbsp;
Saldaña's Neytiri is pretty much sidelined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.320 --> 00:12:38.760
for most of the film's arduous runtime. She&nbsp;
mostly plays the cliché role of the concerned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.760 --> 00:12:42.540
wife and mother, usually in the background&nbsp;
fretting while Jake and her children get to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.540 --> 00:12:46.260
do all the narrative heavy lifting. There&nbsp;
are even some very uncomfortable moments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.260 --> 00:12:50.760
when Jake forcefully shuts her up after&nbsp;
she brings up valid complaints. Yikes.

00:12:50.760 --> 00:12:54.420
To be fair, the sequel shifts the focus&nbsp;
away from Jake as well, since the story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.420 --> 00:12:58.980
is a generational one about their kids, instead&nbsp;
of them. But Jake still gets a lot more to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.980 --> 00:13:02.820
in "The Way of Water," while in the first one,&nbsp;
Neytiri and Jake's importance to the overall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.820 --> 00:13:07.440
narrative was much more equal. Also, while it's&nbsp;
true that Neytiri gets to be an awesome warrior&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.440 --> 00:13:11.400
during the climax, it's also kind of a "too&nbsp;
little, too late" situation at that point.

